/* A BORROWED die - one standing in a lane it only occupies because of a live
   Fair Trade loan - is never a legal Break target either (brief section 1,
   Fair Trade's entry: "Break cannot target a borrowed die at all, full
   stop"). Deliberately built to the SAME SHAPE as _breakPreserved above,
   because that is exactly what the ruling asks for: one predicate, filtered
   in _breakBegin and gated again at the tap in _breakDie.
   WHAT IT CLOSES WAS MEASURED, NOT THEORETICAL. Borrow a die, break it, keep
   the family payout and still have six real dice - 48.6% win rate against
   15.2% for an honest self-break. The alternative that used to sit here,
   brief 4b's "the lane persists, benched die returns immediately, stay at 6",
   tried to PRICE the break instead of forbidding it and is now STRUCK: once
   Fair Trade's swap is involved there is no non-arbitrary answer for which
   die ends up standing where.
   THE SEAT TEST IS THE ONE startPTurn'S LOAN-EXPIRY BLOCK ALREADY USES -
   matchDice[lane] still holding ft.borrowed - so the two can never disagree
   about whether the loan is still standing in its lane. If some other effect
   has taken that seat since (steal_die, a mirror swap) the die there is not
   borrowed and Break may have it. If matchDice cannot be read at all the die
   is treated as borrowed: refusing a legal target costs the player one Break,
   allowing an illegal one costs the exploit above.
   DELIBERATE TARGETING ONLY. Passive death on a borrowed die - Obsidian's own
   shatter - is UNCHANGED by this and still resolves through the Fair-Trade
   branch at the top of _removeDieAt. */
function _breakBorrowed(d){
  if(!d||!G)return false;
  try{
    var ft=G._fairTrade;
    if(!ft||ft.lane===undefined||ft.lane===null)return false;
    if(_laneOf(d)!==ft.lane)return false;
    if(!G.matchDice||ft.lane>=G.matchDice.length)return true;
    return G.matchDice[ft.lane]===ft.borrowed;
  }catch(e){}
  return false;
}
